  "worked_solution": "First, notice that the range of $f(x,y)$ is $[-1,1].$ So, we will consider only $c$-level curves for $c\\in [-1,1].$\n\nThe level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equations \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}\u2061y & =c \\\\ y & =-\\operatorname{arcsin}\u2061c+2\\pi n,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ can be any integer. \n\nNotice that the equations $y=-\\operatorname{arcsin}\u2061c+2\\pi n$ do not depend on $x.$ So, for a given $c\\in [-1,1]$ and integer $n$, the right-hand side of the equation is constant. As a result, we obtain a family of horizontal lines, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069946/assets/67e9cfee7779fdde389a0804e7f52372ca9434de56b6611d296d4ac9c0759f72.png)",
